s `.js` extensions on imports that will be ESM imports.\n\nWait - there's a subtlety. The tsconfig uses Node16 module resolution with type: \"module\". In this mode, TypeScript expects:\n- Relative imports to have `.js` extensions (which get mapped to `.ts` files during compilation)\n- Support for both ESM and CJS\n\nSo if I import from `./routes` in my code, TypeScript with Node16 will expect `./routes.js` (the .js extension maps to .ts during compilation). This is the standard ESM pattern.\n\nLet me now plan the structure:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts      -- exports createApp()\n  app.ts        -- Express app setup\n  middleware/\n    auth.ts     -- Authentication middleware\n  routes/\n    auth.ts     -- Auth routes (register, login)\n    folders.ts  -- Folder routes\n    documents.ts -- Document routes\n    share.ts    -- Share routes\n    activity.ts -- Activity routes\n  services/\n    userService.ts -- User management\n    folderService.ts -- Folder management\n    documentService.ts -- Document management\n    shareService.ts -- Sharing & permissions\n    activityService.ts -- Activity logging\n  types.ts      -- Type definitions\ntests/\n  app.test.ts   -- Main tests\n```\n\nActually, let me keep it simpler with fewer files. The task says to implement a full API. Let me organize it properly but not over-engineer it.\n\nLet me plan the implementation in detail:\n\n**Types:**\n- User: { id, username, password (hashed), createdAt }\n- Folder: { id, name, parentId: string | null, ownerId, createdAt, updatedAt }\n- Document: { id, title, content, folderId, ownerId, createdAt, updatedAt }\n- Permission: { resourceType: 'folder' | 'document', resourceId, userId, role: 'viewer' | 'editor' | 'owner' }\n- ActivityLog: { id, actionType, userId, targetType, targetId, details, timestamp }\n\nWait, \"owner\" is a special role that only the creator has, but the spec says \"Share folders or documents with other users by assigning them a role.\" So you can share with someone and assign them viewer, editor, or owner role? Let me re-read.\n\n\"Three roles: viewer, editor, owner\n- Viewer: can read the resource\n- Editor: can read and modify the resource, and share it with others\n- Owner: full control including deletion and role management\nShare folders or documents with other users by assigning them a role. The creator of a resource is automatically its owner.\"\n\nSo yes, you can share with someone as owner too. The creator automatically gets owner.\n\n\"When a user has access to a resource through multiple paths (e.g., direct share + folder share), their effective permission should reflect the most permissive role granted.\"\n\nSo if a user is viewer on a folder but editor on a document inside it, they get editor on that document. Or if they're viewer on a folder directly but the folder is shared with them as editor through a parent folder, they get editor.\n\nWait, \"folder share\" - when you share a folder with someone, does that give them access to everything inside? I think yes, sharing a folder gives that user the same role on all contents (documents and sub-folders) with the most permissive rule winning.\n\nLet me think about how to model this effectively.\n\nFor authorization checks:\n1. Check if user has direct permission on the resource (via share)\n2. If the resource is a document, check if user has permission on the parent folder (inherited)\n3. If the resource is a folder, check if user has permission on any ancestor folder\n\nThe effective permission is the highest role among all paths.\n\nRole hierarchy: viewer < editor < owner\n\nNow let me think about the routes:\n\nPOST /auth/register -- { username, password } -> { id, username, token }\nPOST /auth/login -- { username, password } -> { token }\nPOST /folders -- { name, parentId? } (auth required) -> folder\nGET /folders/:id (auth required) -> folder with contents\nPATCH /folders/:id -- { name } (auth required, editor+) -> folder\nDELETE /folders/:id (auth required, owne