ally, I want to make sure I handle everything correctly. Let me think through all the edge cases:\n\n**Auth:**\n- Register with existing username -> 409 or 400\n- Register with missing fields -> 400\n- Login with wrong username -> 401\n- Login with wrong password -> 401\n- Request without token -> 401\n- Request with invalid token -> 401\n\n**Folders:**\n- Create folder without name -> 400\n- Create folder with non-existent parentId -> 400 or 404\n- Create folder inside a folder you don't have editor access to -> 403\n- Get folder that doesn't exist -> 404\n- Get folder you don't have access to -> 403\n- Update folder without editor access -> 403\n- Update non-existent folder -> 404\n- Delete folder without owner access -> 403\n- Delete non-existent folder -> 404\n- Delete folder with nested content - should we cascade delete? The spec says \"delete folders\" but doesn't specify cascade. I'll delete the folder only, and documents/folders inside become orphaned? Actually, that's messy. Let me think...\n\nActually, for deleting a folder, the spec just says \"delete folders\". A natural expectation would be to either prevent deletion if not empty, or cascade delete all contents. Given this is a \"permission maze\" task, I think preventing deletion if not empty makes sense, or we could cascade. Let me go with: deleting a folder also deletes all nested content (documents and sub-folders recursively). This is the most user-friendly approach and matches intuitive behavior.\n\nWait, but that's complex. Let me just prevent deletion of non-empty folders. The user must delete contents first. That's simpler and checks permissions on each nested item.\n\nActually, the spec is quite brief about this. Let me just delete the folder - nested resources become orphaned but we're in-memory only so it's fine. Or better yet, let me cascade delete. Let me just do it: delete folder, delete all permissions on that folder, and if it has children folders, recursively delete them too, and delete all documents in it.\n\nLet me keep it simpler: delete the folder. Throw an error if it has children (either sub-folders or documents). The user must clean up first.\n\nHmm actually, I think cascade delete is what users would expect. Let me implement it. When a folder is deleted:\n1. Delete the folder\n2. Delete all documents inside it\n3. Recursively delete sub-folders\n4. Delete all associated permissions\n5. Log the activity\n\n**Documents:**\n- Create document without title/folderId -> 400\n- Create document in non-existent folder -> 404\n- Create document in folder without editor access -> 403\n- Get non-existent document -> 404\n- Get document without viewer access -> 403\n- Update non-existent document -> 404\n- Update document without editor access -> 403\n- Delete non-existent document -> 404\n- Delete document without owner access -> 403\n\n**Sharing:**\n- Share non-existent resource -> 404\n- Share with non-existent user -> 404\n- Share without editor permission on the resource -> 403\n- Share with invalid role -> 400\n- Share same resource+user again (should update role or return error?) - I'll update the role\n\n**Activity:**\n- Just returns all activities sorted by timestamp desc\n\nNow let me think about the permission inheritance more carefully.\n\nFor a document, the effective permission is:\n1. User is the document owner (ownerId) -> owner\n2. User has direct permission on the document -> that role\n3. User has permission on the parent folder -> that role (inherited)\n4. User has permission on grandparent folder -> that role (inherited)\n5. ... etc up the folder tree\n\nThe user's role is the MAXIMUM of all these.\n\nFor a folder, it's similar:\n1. User is the folder owner -> owner\n2. User has direct permission on the folder -> that role\n3. User has permission on parent folder -> that role (inherited)\n4. ... etc\n\nSo effectively, for any resource, I need to compute the effective role by walking up the ancestor chain.\n\nLet me implement this