itance chain is:\n- Document -> parent folder -> grandparent folder -> ... (up the folder tree)\n- Folder -> parent folder -> grandparent folder -> ... (up the folder tree)\n\nThis is what my recursive function implements. Good.\n\nLet me now code this. I'll write everything in a well-structured way.\n\nOne more thing - for `GET /folders/:id`, what should it return? The task says \"get folder\". I'll include:\n- The folder info\n- List of child folders (just ids or full objects?)\n- List of documents in it\n\nActually, for simplicity and to avoid leaking info, let me just return the folder itself and let the user query children separately if needed. Or I could include children info.\n\nWait, the spec says \"Folders can be nested inside other folders (hierarchical structure)\". It doesn't explicitly say to list children in the GET response. Let me just return the folder with its children (sub-folders and documents) included.\n\nOK, I'm overthinking this. Let me start writing code now.\n\nFor the folder structure response:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"...\",\n  \"name\": \"...\",\n  \"parentId\": \"...\",\n  \"ownerId\": \"...\",\n  \"createdAt\": \"...\",\n  \"updatedAt\": \"...\",\n  \"children\": {\n    \"folders\": [...],\n    \"documents\": [...]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, should the user only see resources they have access to? Yes, I think so. When listing children of a folder, only include items the user has at least viewer access to.\n\nActually, that opens up another question. If a folder is shared with me as viewer, do I automatically see all documents in it? Or only the ones I have individual access to?\n\nI think the natural model is: if I have viewer access on a folder, I can see all documents and sub-folders inside it (as viewer). The sharing on the folder cascades to its contents.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: what if a sub-folder inside a shared folder has different permissions? E.g., Folder A is shared with me as viewer. Inside Folder A, there's Folder B shared w